    "summary": "The service is healthy and I made no changes. Here is what I found.\n\n**What I found**\n\n- All 19 pods in `test-hotel-reservation` were Running and Ready with zero restarts. All six PVCs were Bound, and no Warning events were present.\n- The only error in the logs was in the frontend: `grpc/lb/consul: error retrieving instances from Consul ... connection refused` for `srv-search`, `srv-profile`, and `srv-recommendation`. All seven of these errors are timestamped <time>\u2013<time>, which is before Consul began serving on port 8500. Consul's logs show frontend health queries succeeding from <time> on, and the frontend has logged no errors since.\n- I checked the Consul Service and Endpoints. Both map port 8500 to the Consul pod at 10.244.1.80, which is correct.\n\n**Functional checks through the frontend (all passed)**\n\n- Search, `/hotels`: returned hotels from geo, rate, and profile.\n- Recommendations, `require=dis` and `require=price`: both returned 200 with results.\n- User login: \"Login successfully!\"\n- Reservation: \"Reserve successfully!\"\n\nOne recommendations call initially returned 400. That was my mistake: I used `require=dist`, but the valid values are `dis`, `rate`, and `price`.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe anomaly was a transient startup race between the frontend and Consul. It has resolved on its own, and all three supported operations work. I did not change any resources, because nothing needed mitigation. If the frontend's startup errors matter for your alerting, a rolling restart of `deploy/frontend` would clear them from the logs, but it is not required.",
